text:
marta: hey all — quick onboarding question. i'm setting up a small local project + CI job that calls the hosted API. i'm unclear which key we should use where, and i'm already hitting intermittent 429s when testing streaming locally. what's the recommended env var names and backoff strategy? also do sandbox and hosted quotas behave differently for streaming requests?

devon: welcome! :tada: short answers — local/dev use SANDBOX_API_KEY, prod/qa use HOSTED_API_KEY. CI: prefer ephemeral tokens or a CI-scoped hosted key with limited scope. sandbox quotas are smaller and have stricter streaming concurrency limits, so you can see 429s if you open many streams.

sam_k: +1 to what devon said. some extra practical tips:
- env var names we standardize on: `SANDBOX_API_KEY`, `HOSTED_API_KEY`, and `RW_EPHEMERAL_TOKEN` for short-lived credentials.
- we surface rate-limit info in headers. always check `retry-after` when present.
- for streaming-specific 429s, the typical reasons are: too many concurrent connections from the same IP or token, or hitting the per-minute token quota for streaming.

jules: if you want the CI/infra team to whitelist your CI runner IPs for higher sandbox concurrency, ping `@infra-team` with the runner ranges and a short justification. that usually speeds up troubleshooting.

marta: thanks — can you paste an example of a small backoff/retry snippet? i want to implement something straightforward in node.

sam_k: sure, here's what we recommend (exponential with jitter + honoring retry-after):

```js
// pseudo-code
async function callWithBackoff(fn, maxAttempts = 6) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < maxAttempts) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      const retryAfter = parseInt(err.headers && err.headers['retry-after']);
      if (retryAfter) {
        await sleep(retryAfter * 1000);
      } else {
        const base = Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100; // ms
        const jitter = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100);
        await sleep(base + jitter);
      }
      attempt += 1;
      if (attempt === maxAttempts) throw err;
    }
  }
}
```

sam_k: also when streaming, avoid reconnect loops that immediately resume on every chunk/error. back off to at least a few seconds before re-opening a stream.

marta: good call. for local dev, i'm using a proxy to multiplex streams on a single connection — could that trigger limits?

devon: yes, multiplexing can look like many concurrent streams depending on how the proxy maps them to backend connections. if the proxy uses a single egress IP, the platform may throttle based on per-IP concurrency. Worth raising with infra if you need sustained concurrency for local testing.

jules: one more operational thing — if you see repeated 429s, include these in the support ticket: sample request id (x-rw-request-id), the token type (sandbox/hosted), IP address, and a short repro. it helps us rule out infra issues vs quota.

marta: where do i request an increase for sandbox quotas?

infra-team: file a request in the #infra-requests board (or ping us here). include: your workspace, token name, CI runner IPs (if applicable), approximate RPS and concurrency, and why you need higher sandbox concurrency. we typically review within 1 business day.

marta: perfect. last question — any naming conventions for env vars in repos to avoid accidental prod key usage?

devon: we keep a small script in our standard repo template that fails CI if `HOSTED_API_KEY` is present in plain text or env files. locals should use `.env.local` with `SANDBOX_API_KEY`. also we encourage using `RW_KEY_ENV=SANDBOX` or `RW_KEY_ENV=HOSTED` so code can assert allowed modes at startup.

ops-bot: reminder: do not commit API keys to git. Scanning rules will auto-open a PR revert if a key is detected.

marta: awesome — thanks everyone, this helps a lot. i'll open an infra request for our CI IPs and switch to the backoff snippet for streaming. :thumbsup:

devon: welcome aboard again! ping if you want a quick walkthrough of token rotation flows.

sam_k: happy to pair on test harnesses if you want.

jules: reach out if the infra request stalls, i'll escalate.

marta: will do — marking this resolved for now. cheers!
